{
  "passage": "The heart is a muscular organ about the size of your fist. It lies in the chest, slightly towards the left, and it pumps blood to all parts of the body without stopping, all through our lives. The human heart has four chambers. The two upper chambers are called atria and the two lower chambers are called ventricles. The right side of the heart receives blood that has given its oxygen to the body and sends it to the lungs. In the lungs the blood picks up fresh oxygen and gets rid of carbon dioxide. This oxygen-rich blood returns to the left side of the heart, which pumps it to the rest of the body. The wall between the left and right sides keeps the two kinds of blood from mixing. Valves between the chambers allow blood to flow in only one direction. Blood leaves the heart through arteries, which have thick, elastic walls because the blood in them is under high pressure. Blood returns to the heart through veins, which have thinner walls and contain valves of their own. Each contraction of the heart is a heartbeat, and the push of blood in the arteries can be felt as the pulse at the wrist. A healthy adult at rest has a pulse rate of about 72 beats per minute, while for a small child it is higher. During exercise, the heart beats faster to supply more oxygen to the muscles.",
